      "heading": "Clinical Evidence: What Does Research Say About This Supplement Stack for Blood Sugar?",
      "body": "<p>Clinical trials and systematic reviews provide evidence that each component of the stack can modestly improve blood sugar metrics. A 2024 systematic review of chromium supplementation reported a small but statistically significant reduction in fasting glucose and glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) among adults with type 2 diabetes (Georgaki et al., 2024). A 2024 randomized crossover trial found that cinnamon spice reduced continuous glucose monitoring readings by an average of 0.3 mmol/L during the post‑prandial period in adults with prediabetes (Zelicha et al., 2024). Moringa oleifera leaf supplementation in a 2021 study improved fasting glucose and HbA1c in subjects with prediabetes (Gómez‑Martínez et al., 2021). Magnesium supplementation in a 2024 trial lowered insulin resistance indices in women with PCOS, a condition closely linked to type 2 diabetes (Shahmoradi et al., 2024). Finally, a 2025 randomized placebo‑controlled trial of prebiotic soluble fiber and anthocyanin showed improved fasting glucose and lipid profiles in patients with type 2 diabetes (Teparak et al., 2025). The evidence ranges from meta‑analyses to small RCTs, indicating that while the effects are generally modest, they are consistent across multiple nutrients. The strength of evidence is strongest for chromium and cinnamon, moderate for magnesium and moringa, and emerging for prebiotic fiber and anthocyanin. Action tip: Ask your healthcare provider which lab tests (e.g., HbA1c, fasting glucose) and monitoring schedule are right for you.</p>\n",
