      "heading": "Dosage and How to Use Best Supplement Stacking for Type 2 Diabetes for Blood Sugar",
      "body": "<p>Chromium, cinnamon, magnesium, moringa, and prebiotic fiber plus anthocyanin have each been tested for blood sugar effects — but in separate clinical trials, each looking at one ingredient on its own. No published trial has tested this exact five‑ingredient combination together, so there is no single \"stack dose\" backed by research. Instead, the table below lists the specific amount and duration used in the trial behind each ingredient, so you can compare what you’re taking against what was actually studied.</p>\n<p><strong>Doses Used in the Supporting Trials</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table class=\"md-table\"><thead><tr><th class=\"tbl-th\">Ingredient</th><th class=\"tbl-th\">Dose Studied</th><th class=\"tbl-th\">Trial Duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Chromium (trivalent, e.g. picolinate)</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">50–1000 mcg/day</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">2–6 months (across 15 pooled RCTs)</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Cinnamon (whole spice)</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">4 g/day</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">4 weeks</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Magnesium (oxide)</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">250 mg/day</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">2 months</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Moringa oleifera leaf powder</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">2.4 g/day (six 400 mg capsules)</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">12 weeks</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Prebiotic fiber + anthocyanin</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">2.8 g/day (eight 350 mg capsules, split across meals and bedtime)</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">60 days</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"tbl-source\">Sources: Georgaki et al., 2024 (chromium); Zelicha et al., 2024 (cinnamon); Shahmoradi et al., 2024 (magnesium); Gómez‑Martínez et al., 2021 (moringa); Teparak et al., 2025 (prebiotic fiber + anthocyanin).</p>\n<p><strong>A label‑reading note on chromium:</strong> the effective range in trials is measured in micrograms (mcg), not milligrams (mg) — 50–1000 mcg is 0.05–1 mg. Some supplement labels list the weight of the compound (e.g., \"chromium picolinate\") rather than the elemental chromium it contains, which can make doses look larger than they are. Check the Supplement Facts panel for the elemental chromium amount specifically, and don’t assume a higher milligram number on the front of the bottle means a higher chromium dose.</p>\n<p>Because these come from five separate trials rather than one combination study, a reasonable approach is to introduce one ingredient at a time, a few days to a week apart, at the dose shown above. This makes it much easier to identify which ingredient (if any) is behind a side effect, rather than guessing at a combined blend. Action tip: Track any new symptoms against which ingredient you most recently added.</p>\n",
